THE CROSS-CHECK HELD ON EVERY LEG: each R_ mutant left rldimi at 27/27 and each D_ mutant left rlwimi at 28/28.

WHY THERE IS A table2b/.  Table 2's driver passed `${leg#R_}' (REV_COND) to mut_rlwimi_t2.py, whose LEGMAP is
keyed M_REV_COND, so five of the six R_ legs were REFUSED -- recorded in SCORES.txt as "MUTATOR REFUSED -- leg
NOT measured", never as a score.  R_NULL has its own branch and ran.  killtable2b.sh passes `M_${leg#R_}',
re-runs BASE plus the five refused legs in a separate clone (/tmp/gxppc2b), and writes into table2b/ so that
table 2's logs -- including the five refusal logs, mutate_R_*.txt -- are preserved as they were.

THE ONE MISS, stated as what it is.  PRETABLE predicted D_NOCIRC at 18/27, reasoning that its census "aborts at
182 of 495 cells" as in table 1 and so reds `census run' and `disasm total' under their new literal pins.  This
time the census RAN ALL 495 CELLS (511/511 words disassembled) and both rows were green: 20/27, which is table
1's 19/27 plus `census run'.  The cause is in the logs: table 1's probe_M_NOCIRC.raw says
"CENSUS SESSION ABORTED how=TIMEOUT at base=182" -- the 90 s per-session timeout -- and table 2's run took
394.0 s in total without one.  Under this mutant a census session takes close to that timeout, so whether it
aborts depends on load.  THE PIN IS NOT LOAD-SENSITIVE ON A CORRECT BUILD: BASE runs the whole probe in 0.1 s,
and D_NOCIRC fails the gate either way, on `census wrong' (237 cells).  So the miss is a wrong premise carried
from table 1, not a probe behaviour -- and it made the probe's own CONTROL_ROWS comment ("whose census hangs and
aborts at 182 of 495 cells") a wrong record.  That comment is corrected in regress/ppc_rldimi_probe.py; the
shipped probe therefore differs from base_20260912/ppc_rldimi_probe.py.frozen by that comment ONLY (three
comment lines replace one), which is the instrument this table ran.

A LITERAL-PIN NOTE: the leg that was meant to test the new `disasm total' / `census run' literal pins did not,
because the census completed.  Those pins are exercised only by a census that ends short, and no leg in this
table produced one.  They remain pins by construction (both compare against a literal, 511/511 and 495), not by
a measured kill.
